# Loot tables

Loot tables are JSON files used to randomly generating items, located at `data/<namespace>/loot_table`.
On the vanilla gameplay they are used on chest loot from structures, mob drops, block drops, archeology, fishing, among others.
They can be used to override these or create new ones.

## JSON format

For information about the JSON format in general, see the [JSON](/wiki/info/json) page.

The loot table JSON object can have the following keys:

- `type`: used to ensure all parameters are valid, most common values for this are `chest`, `entity`, `block`, and `generic`, but can be omitted.
- `random_sequence`
- `functions`: list of item modifiers that will affect all the generated items
- `pools`: a list with entries of the loot table.
  - `rolls`: number of times this pool is rolled, having a value of 2 is equivalent of running 2 times a command of a loot table of `rolls` value of 1.
  - `bonus_rolls`: number provider used to reroll more times this pool, depends on the luck attribute of the player.
  - `conditions`: list of predicates that must succeed, or no items will be generated.
  - `functions`: list of item modifiers that will affect the generated items of this roll.
  - `entries`: list of entries of the loot table, which items will be given to the player.
    - `type`: specifies what will be given to the player, can be `item`, `alternatives`, `dynamic`, `loot_table`, `sequence`, `slots`, `empty`, and `tag`.
      - `weight`: how likely is it to land this roll on this entry, higher values mean it's more likely. For example, having a loot table with a diamond of weight 1 and a dirt block with weight 99 means the chance of getting the diamond block is 1% but getting the dirt block is 99%.
      - `quality`: increases the weight based on luck.
      - `conditions`: list of predicates that must succeed, or no items will be generated on this entry only.
      - `functions`: list of item modifiers that will affect the generated items of this entry only.

## `entries`

`item`: has `name`, that is an item ID:

```json:diamond.json
{
  "pools": [
    {
      "rolls": 1,
      "entries": [
        {
          "type": "minecraft:item",
          "name": "minecraft:diamond"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

`alternatives`: has `children`, this entry only returns the first children that succeeds, this is used in the vanilla datapack to drop stone if the pickaxe has silk touch, but drop cobblestone otherwise:

```json:minecraft:blocks/stone.json
{
  "type": "minecraft:block",
  "pools": [
    {
      "entries": [
        {
          "type": "minecraft:alternatives",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "minecraft:item",
              "conditions": [
                {
                  "condition": "minecraft:match_tool",
                  "predicate": {
                    "predicates": {
                      "minecraft:enchantments": [
                        {
                          "enchantments": "minecraft:silk_touch",
                          "levels": {
                            "min": 1
                          }
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  }
                }
              ],
              "name": "minecraft:stone"
            },
            {
              "type": "minecraft:item",
              "conditions": [
                {
                  "condition": "minecraft:survives_explosion"
                }
              ],
              "name": "minecraft:cobblestone"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "rolls": 1
    }
  ],
  "random_sequence": "minecraft:blocks/stone"
}
```
`empty`: it drops nothing

`group`: has `children` which are more entries but are grouped, which is convenient to apply the same condition predicate to all.

`loot_table`: has `value` which is a resource location of another loot table or a loot table object, for example, a loot table that rolls once the which loot table

```json:namespace:witch.json
{
  "pools": [
    {
      "rolls": 1,
      "entries": [
        {
          "type": "minecraft:loot_table",
          "value": "minecraft:entities/witch"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

`squence`: returns all `children` until there is one which condition isn't meet.

You can use [Misode's generator](https://misode.github.io/loot-table/) in order to generate your loot table

# More examples

The following example has a 20% chance of giving a diamond, 40% chance for a gold ingot and 40% for an emerald and it's rolled 5 times.

```json:random_minerals.json
{
  "pools": [
    {
      "rolls": 5,
      "entries": [
        {
          "type": "minecraft:item",
          "name": "diamond",
          "weight": 20
        },
        {
          "type": "minecraft:item",
          "name": "gold_ingot",
          "weight": 40
        },
        {
          "type": "minecraft:item",
          "name": "emerald",
          "weight": 40
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

The following example gives a diamond if the condition succeeds, in this case, it's a `time_check` predicate, so it must be night

```json:diamond_at_night.json
{
  "pools": [
    {
      "rolls": 1,
      "entries": [
        {
          "type": "minecraft:item",
          "name": "diamond"
        }
      ],
      "conditions": [
        {
          "condition": "minecraft:time_check",
          "clock": "minecraft:overworld",
          "value": {
            "min": 12000,
            "max": 24000
          },
          "period": 24000
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

## Generating loot tables in-game

Loot tables can be generated from:
- `/loot` command: `loot <give <players>|insert <target_pos>|spawn <target_pos>|replace <block|entity> <fish ...|loot <loot_table>|kill <target>|mine ...>`
- Advancement `rewards` object can specify a `loot` entry which is a list of loot table files
- Overriding vanilla loot tables by having the file on the same path as the vanilla path, for example, to override the zombie drops, you would create the file on `data/minecraft/entities/zombie.json`
- `LootTable` NBT for container blocks or `DeathLootTable` for entities, for example `setblock ~ ~ ~ chest{LootTable:"namespace:loot_table"}`